From: ios-milanf-port (desk phase, 2026-10-25)
Subject: [PATCH] MILANF: the CPU driver, with the topology read off the unit's own DTB

This is the first driver in the set whose SoC-specific surface turns out to be
**zero**. The only constant in it is the PSCI function ID 0xC4000003 for
CPU_ON, and that is SMCCC - a numbered function in a firmware calling
convention, not a memory-mapped address - so it is the same number on any
PSCI 0.2+ implementation, on any SoC. Everything else is either architected
(Aff1/Aff0 in MPIDR, ICC_SGI1R_EL1, the GIC redistributor SGI enable) or read
from the topology that ml_topology builds out of the ADT.

So the interesting question was not "does this hardware exist" but "does this
board's topology look like the one the validation code assumes". It was read
off the unit's own DTB rather than assumed, with a new tools/fdt-cpus.py:

    /cpus/cpu@0      reg=0x0 0x0     enable-method=psci
    /cpus/cpu@100    reg=0x0 0x100   ...
    /cpus/cpu@200    reg=0x0 0x200
    /cpus/cpu@300    reg=0x0 0x300
    /cpus/cpu@400    reg=0x0 0x400
    /cpus/cpu@500    reg=0x0 0x500
    /cpus/cpu@600    reg=0x0 0x600
    /cpus/cpu@700    reg=0x0 0x700

Eight cores, Aff1 = 0x0-0x7, Aff0 = 0 on every one, enable-method psci. That
is the same shape the sweet code validates (`phys_id > 0x700 || (phys_id &
0xff) != 0` -> panic) so the check passes unchanged, and it has to: the
cpu-map in the same tree splits them cluster0/core0..core5 and
cluster1/core0..core1, which is the little/big split ml_topology will derive.
The `num_cpus > SWEET_SMP_MAX_CPUS` bound is 8 on both boards.

The match string, though, is different and is the one real edit: the tree's
cpu nodes say `compatible = "qcom,kryo"`, but that is not what IOKit matches
against - it matches the ADT, and tools/mkadt-milanf.py publishes
`qcom,kryo660` on those nodes (CPU_COMPATIBLE, line 116). So the personality
carries qcom,kryo660 while sweet's carries qcom,kryo470. A driver that
compiled and matched nothing would look exactly like a CPU driver that works,
right up to the point where nothing ever calls start().

Two board macros had to be dealt with rather than duplicated:
`SWEET_SMP_MAX_CPUS` and `SWEET_IPI_SGI` live in pexpert/arm64/SWEET.h, and
milanf's header publishes the same values as MILANF_SMP_MAX_CPUS /
MILANF_IPI_SGI instead. Rather than wrap each of the three use sites in a
nested #if, the file gets a small alias block at the top - the same idea as the
nested form already used at machine_routines.c:962 and pe_fiq.c:172, just
hoisted so the body stays board-neutral. The CPU name string goes the same
way: "Kryo 660" on milanf, "Kryo 470" on sweet, because userland reads it out
of the IORegistry.

**Both of this driver's cross-file dependencies were already widened**, which
is why it can be enabled now rather than after more plumbing:

  - `sweet_secondary_entry` is defined in osfmk/arm64/start.s:282, inside
    `#if defined(SWEET) || defined(MILANF)` - the physical PSCI CPU_ON entry
    point, MMU and caches off, with the EL2 fallback.
  - `sweet_cpu_signal_is_enabled` is osfmk/arm64/cpu.c:966, same widened
    guard - the bring-up observer that waits for the self-SGI to clear
    SIGPdisabled.

Checked and left alone, because it looks like a gap and is not one:
pe_fiq.c:109 reads `virtual-timer-intid` from the device tree under a guard
that is still sweet-only, so milanf skips it and keeps the compiled-in
default. That default is 19 on both boards (SWEET.h:67 and MILANF.h), and the
unit's FDT has no `virtual-timer-intid` property at all - a scan of every
property name in the tree finds none - so the skipped read would have failed
and fallen through to the same 19. If that ever changes it is a one-line
guard widen, not a value to guess.

Verified without a compiler: apply --check exit 0, then a real apply to the
ci-tail scratch tree - both files cleanly, CR=0 in sources and patch. Link
unverified; CI in the morning.

diff --git a/iokit/Kernel/arm/SweetCPU.cpp b/iokit/Kernel/arm/SweetCPU.cpp
index 2545cf9..b167484 100644
--- a/iokit/Kernel/arm/SweetCPU.cpp
+++ b/iokit/Kernel/arm/SweetCPU.cpp
@@ -1,11 +1,13 @@
 /*
- * A55 SMP bring-up for sweet using XNU's legacy IOCPU interface and PSCI.
+ * SMP bring-up for the Qualcomm boards (sweet / SM7150 Kryo 470,
+ * milanf / SM6375 Kryo 660) using XNU's legacy IOCPU interface and PSCI.
  * The architected timer and SGI1 already enter through pe_fiq/sleh_irq.
- * CPU_OFF, suspend and the A76 cores still require separate platform work.
+ * CPU_OFF, suspend and the big-cluster cores still require separate
+ * platform work.
  */
 #include <pexpert/arm64/board_config.h>
 
-#if defined(SWEET)
+#if defined(SWEET) || defined(MILANF)
 #include <IOKit/IOCPU.h>
 #include <IOKit/IOLib.h>
 #include <libkern/c++/OSData.h>
@@ -18,6 +20,22 @@
 #include <mach/mach_time.h>
 #include <pexpert/pexpert.h>
 
+/*
+ * Board parameters. Both boards publish these under their own names
+ * in pexpert/arm64/SWEET.h and MILANF.h; these aliases keep the rest
+ * of the file board-neutral. The name string is what userland reads
+ * out of the IORegistry.
+ */
+#if defined(MILANF)
+#define SWEET_CPU_MAX_CPUS   MILANF_SMP_MAX_CPUS
+#define SWEET_CPU_IPI_SGI    MILANF_IPI_SGI
+#define SWEET_CPU_NAME       "Kryo 660"
+#else
+#define SWEET_CPU_MAX_CPUS   SWEET_SMP_MAX_CPUS
+#define SWEET_CPU_IPI_SGI    SWEET_IPI_SGI
+#define SWEET_CPU_NAME       "Kryo 470"
+#endif
+
 extern "C" void sweet_secondary_entry(void);
 extern "C" boolean_t sweet_cpu_signal_is_enabled(unsigned int cpu);
 
@@ -73,16 +91,21 @@ SweetCPU::start(IOService *provider)
 	if (*(const uint32_t *)reg->getBytesNoCopy() != cpu->phys_id) {
 		return false;
 	}
-	/* MPIDR Aff1 = core: 0x000-0x500 A55, 0x600/0x700 A76 */
+	/*
+	 * MPIDR Aff1 = core: 0x000-0x500 little cluster, 0x600/0x700 big
+	 * cluster. Read off each board's own DTB: sweet is Kryo 470
+	 * (A55/A76), milanf is Kryo 660 (A55/A78), and both put Aff0 = 0
+	 * on all eight cores.
+	 */
 	if (cpu->phys_id > 0x700 || (cpu->phys_id & 0xff) != 0 ||
-	    topology->num_cpus > SWEET_SMP_MAX_CPUS) {
+	    topology->num_cpus > SWEET_CPU_MAX_CPUS) {
 		panic("SWEET CPU: invalid topology cpu=%u mpidr=0x%x count=%u",
 		    cpu->cpu_id, cpu->phys_id, topology->num_cpus);
 	}
 	if (!IOCPU::start(provider)) {
 		return false;
 	}
-	cpuName = OSSymbol::withCStringNoCopy("Kryo 470");
+	cpuName = OSSymbol::withCStringNoCopy(SWEET_CPU_NAME);
 	if (cpuName == NULL) {
 		return false;
 	}
@@ -129,7 +152,7 @@ SweetCPU::startAll(void *arg __unused, wait_result_t result __unused)
 	uint32_t requested = 1;
 	PE_parse_boot_argn("cpus", &requested, sizeof(requested));
 	IOLog("SWEET CPU: requested=%u selected=%u (limit=%u)\n",
-	    requested, topology->num_cpus, SWEET_SMP_MAX_CPUS);
+	    requested, topology->num_cpus, SWEET_CPU_MAX_CPUS);
 
 	uint64_t deadline;
 	nanoseconds_to_deadline(10 * NSEC_PER_SEC, &deadline);
@@ -223,7 +246,7 @@ SweetCPU::signalCPU(IOCPU *target)
 	uint64_t sgi = (((mpidr >> 32) & 0xff) << 48) |
 	    (((mpidr >> 16) & 0xff) << 32) |
 	    (((mpidr >> 8) & 0xff) << 16) |
-	    ((uint64_t)SWEET_IPI_SGI << 24) | (1ULL << aff0);
+	    ((uint64_t)SWEET_CPU_IPI_SGI << 24) | (1ULL << aff0);
 	__builtin_arm_dsb(DSB_ISHST);
 	__builtin_arm_wsr64("ICC_SGI1R_EL1", sgi);
 	__builtin_arm_isb(ISB_SY);
@@ -268,4 +291,4 @@ SweetCPU::getCPUName(void)
 {
 	return cpuName;
 }
-#endif /* SWEET */
+#endif /* SWEET || MILANF */
diff --git a/iokit/KernelConfigTables.cpp b/iokit/KernelConfigTables.cpp
index 813de74..757aeb1 100644
--- a/iokit/KernelConfigTables.cpp
+++ b/iokit/KernelConfigTables.cpp
@@ -84,12 +84,13 @@ const char * gIOKernelConfigTables =
     "   },"
 #elif defined(ARM64_BOARD_CONFIG_MILANF)
     /*
-     * milanf gets the platform expert plus the two services a device with
-     * no passcode and no reset path needs: AppleKeyStore and IOWatchdog.
-     * Every other Sweet* driver is still an empty translation unit on this
-     * board, and a personality whose class does not exist only earns
-     * an IOKit "can't find class" line. They come back one at a time,
-     * each with its own board values and its own personality here.
+     * milanf gets the platform expert, the CPU driver, and the two
+     * services a device with no passcode and no reset path needs:
+     * AppleKeyStore and IOWatchdog. Every other Sweet* driver is still
+     * an empty translation unit on this board, and a personality
+     * whose class does not exist only earns an IOKit "can't find
+     * class" line. They come back one at a time, each with its own
+     * board values and its own personality here.
      *
      * IONameMatch is "xiaomi,sweet" and that is deliberate, not a
      * copy-paste slip: the milanf ADT root compatible list is
@@ -105,6 +106,12 @@ const char * gIOKernelConfigTables =
     "     'IOProbeScore'    = 10000:32;"
     "   },"
     "   {"
+    "     'IOClass'         = SweetCPU;"
+    "     'IOProviderClass' = IOPlatformDevice;"
+    "     'IONameMatch'     = 'qcom,kryo660';"
+    "     'IOProbeScore'    = 10000:32;"
+    "   },"
+    "   {"
     "     'IOClass'         = AppleKeyStore;"
     "     'IOProviderClass' = IOResources;"
     "     'IOMatchCategory' = AppleKeyStore;"
